<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0">
  <head>
    <title>Problem-First Mind Map</title>
  </head>
  <body>

    <!-- EXAMPLE 1: REPORTING -->
    <outline text="📊 Manual reporting: 10h/week per person">
      <outline text="Context: SQL + SharePoint data, no dev skills in team, M365 E5 licenses, templates already tried">

        <outline text="✅ Automated dashboard">
          <outline text="Eliminates manual work. Data always fresh. Scales without effort.">
            <outline text="Trade-off: 2-3 weeks setup, needs someone who knows the data model" />
          </outline>

          <outline text="Which tool?">
            <outline text="✅ Power BI">
              <outline text="Licenses included in E5. IT supports it. Non-devs can build reports after training.">
                <outline text="Trade-off: Less flexible for real-time. Desktop app needed for authoring." />
              </outline>

              <outline text="How to connect data?">
                <outline text="✅ Import mode + scheduled refresh">
                  <outline text="Fast dashboards (data local). Daily refresh enough — was daily Excel anyway." />
                  <outline text="Trade-off: Data up to 24h old." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ DirectQuery (real-time)">
                  <outline text="Each click hits DB. Adds production load. Overkill for daily reporting." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Operational dashboards needing last 5 min of data." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ Dataflow (ETL in Power BI)">
                  <outline text="Requires Premium. Only worth it for 5+ teams sharing same data model." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Multiple teams need one governed data layer." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>

            <outline text="❌ Grafana">
              <outline text="Nobody knows it. Needs self-hosting. Built for ops monitoring, not business reports." />
              <outline text="When it works: Infrastructure monitoring where real-time matters." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="❌ Custom-built (React + API)">
              <outline text="3 months to build. Needs ongoing dev maintenance. We need a solution in weeks, not quarters." />
              <outline text="When it works: Customer-facing dashboard or features no BI tool supports (custom workflows, integrations)." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>

        <outline text="❌ Hire a reporting person">
          <outline text="Expensive. Doesn't scale. Single point of failure. The problem is the process, not the headcount." />
          <outline text="When it works: Reports need expert commentary that can't be automated." />
        </outline>
        <outline text="❌ Better Excel template with macros">
          <outline text="Cuts time from 2h to maybe 1h. Still manual. Still error-prone. Macros break when data format changes." />
          <outline text="When it works: Quick win to buy time while dashboard is being built." />
        </outline>

      </outline>
    </outline>

    <!-- EXAMPLE 2: SIM ACTIVATION -->
    <outline text="📱 SIM activation: 24h → minutes">
      <outline text="Context: Legacy SOAP backend, 800 activations/day manual, 3% error rate, customers switching to competitor">

        <outline text="✅ REST API with real-time provisioning">
          <outline text="Fully automated. Scales linearly. Customer gets instant result.">
            <outline text="Trade-off: Legacy SOAP backend can't be exposed directly." />
          </outline>

          <outline text="How to handle legacy?">
            <outline text="✅ SOAP-to-REST wrapper">
              <outline text="Legacy system untouched (risky to change). Clean REST API for new portal. 2-3 sprints.">
                <outline text="Trade-off: Extra layer to maintain. Wrapper needs update if SOAP changes." />
              </outline>

              <outline text="How to handle failures?">
                <outline text="✅ Async with retry queue">
                  <outline text="Request accepted immediately, queued. Retries handle 95% of transient failures. Customer never sees error page." />
                  <outline text="Trade-off: Not instant (30s-2min). Need status page or push notification." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ Synchronous (fail = error to customer)">
                  <outline text="3% failure rate = 24 failed customers/day seeing error pages. Worse than call center." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Backend is 99.9%+ reliable with &lt;2s response time." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ Manual fallback for all failures">
                  <outline text="Back to manual for 3% of cases. Call center still needed at full capacity." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Last-resort escalation after retry queue exhausted (3 retries, 10 min)." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>

            <outline text="❌ Replace SOAP system entirely">
              <outline text="6-12 months. Handles billing, number porting, 15 downstream systems. Too much risk." />
              <outline text="When it works: Long-term strategic initiative once wrapper proves business case." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="❌ Direct SOAP calls from frontend">
              <outline text="Exposes legacy to public internet. No auth, rate limiting, or error translation." />
              <outline text="When it works: Internal tools behind VPN where security is less critical." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>

        <outline text="❌ Batch processing overnight">
          <outline text="Customer still waits 24h. This IS the problem we're solving." />
          <outline text="When it works: Bulk enterprise orders (500 SIMs) where reliability &gt; speed." />
        </outline>
        <outline text="❌ Manual processing via call center">
          <outline text="This is what we already do. 800/day, 3% error rate. The process IS the problem." />
          <outline text="When it works: Edge cases and VIP customers where automation fails." />
        </outline>

      </outline>
    </outline>

    <!-- EXAMPLE 3: E-COMMERCE SEARCH -->
    <outline text="🔍 E-commerce search: 6-8 seconds per query">
      <outline text="Context: 500K product catalog, SQL LIKE queries on main DB, mobile conversion 40% below desktop, holiday season in 6 weeks">

        <outline text="✅ Dedicated search engine">
          <outline text="Elasticsearch indexes catalog. Fuzzy matching, typo tolerance, faceted filtering. Results under 200ms.">
            <outline text="Trade-off: New infrastructure to maintain. Data sync between DB and search index needed." />
          </outline>

          <outline text="How to sync product data?">
            <outline text="✅ Event-driven sync">
              <outline text="Product changes publish events. Consumer updates index in near real-time. Searchable within seconds.">
                <outline text="Trade-off: Needs message queue (RabbitMQ, Kafka). More moving parts than a cron job." />
              </outline>

              <outline text="How to roll out safely?">
                <outline text="✅ Shadow mode + gradual cutover">
                  <outline text="New search runs in parallel but hidden. Compare results. Route 10% → 50% → 100% when confident." />
                  <outline text="Trade-off: Running two search systems for a few weeks. More operational overhead during transition." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ Big bang release">
                  <outline text="If anything breaks during peak, all customers affected. Rollback = back to slow search. Too risky before holidays." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Low-traffic apps where brief downtime is acceptable and rollback is instant." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ A/B test indefinitely">
                  <outline text="Half of customers still get slow search during peak. Maintaining two systems permanently is expensive." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Genuinely unsure if new approach is better and need statistical proof." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>

            <outline text="❌ Full re-index nightly">
              <outline text="New products not searchable until morning. Price changes stale up to 24h. Lost sales during holiday season." />
              <outline text="When it works: Static catalogs that change weekly or less (B2B parts, document archives)." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="❌ Dual-write from application">
              <outline text="If search index write fails, data inconsistent. App coupled to two storage systems. Every update path needs changes." />
              <outline text="When it works: Prototypes or MVPs where simplicity matters more than reliability." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>

        <outline text="❌ Add database indexes and optimize queries">
          <outline text="SQL full-text better than LIKE but can't do fuzzy matching, typo tolerance, or relevance tuning. Ceiling at 500K products." />
          <outline text="When it works: Smaller catalogs (under 50K) where basic keyword matching is sufficient." />
        </outline>
        <outline text="❌ Pre-computed search results">
          <outline text="Long-tail queries (product names, SKUs, misspellings) are unpredictable. Can't pre-compute every search." />
          <outline text="When it works: 80%+ of searches are the same 100 queries (category browsing, not free-text)." />
        </outline>

      </outline>
    </outline>

    <!-- EXAMPLE 4: EMPLOYEE ONBOARDING -->
    <outline text="🧑‍💼 Employee onboarding: 2 weeks across 6 departments, 30% missing equipment on day one">
      <outline text="Context: 6 departments each own a checklist, no single system of record, 40 new hires/month, IT and facilities tickets created manually, M365 available">

        <outline text="✅ Standardized digital checklist with workflow automation">
          <outline text="One checklist triggers all departments in parallel. Tasks auto-assigned with deadlines. Nothing falls through the cracks.">
            <outline text="Trade-off: Requires all 6 departments to agree on one process. Needs a process owner to maintain it." />
          </outline>

          <outline text="How to implement the workflow?">
            <outline text="✅ Low-code platform (Power Automate + SharePoint)">
              <outline text="Already licensed under M365. HR creates the list, Power Automate notifies each department and tracks completion. No developers needed. Live in 2-3 weeks." />
              <outline text="Trade-off: Limited customization. Complex conditional logic (e.g., different flows for contractors vs. full-time) requires workarounds." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="❌ Custom-built HR portal">
              <outline text="3-4 months of development. Needs a dedicated team to build and maintain. Overkill for a workflow problem that off-the-shelf tools can solve." />
              <outline text="When it works: 500+ hires/month with complex compliance requirements (e.g., background checks, certifications) that no low-code tool can handle." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="❌ Outsource to HR SaaS vendor (BambooHR, Workday)">
              <outline text="€15-30K/year licensing. 3-6 month implementation with vendor consultants. Solves a problem we can fix in 3 weeks with existing tools." />
              <outline text="When it works: Company-wide HR transformation where onboarding is just one module alongside payroll, performance reviews, and benefits." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>

        <outline text="❌ Hire a dedicated onboarding coordinator">
          <outline text="Adds €45-55K/year in salary. Single point of failure during vacation or sick leave. Scales linearly — 2× hires needs 2× coordinators. The problem is the process, not the headcount." />
          <outline text="When it works: White-glove onboarding for senior executives or regulated roles where personal guidance is legally required." />
        </outline>
        <outline text="❌ Better email templates and shared spreadsheet">
          <outline text="Cuts coordination time from hours to maybe 45 minutes. Still manual. Still relies on people checking email. Spreadsheet goes stale by day two. Same 30% equipment miss rate." />
          <outline text="When it works: Quick fix to buy time while the automated workflow is being built. Useful as the interim step, not the solution." />
        </outline>

      </outline>
    </outline>

    <!-- EXAMPLE 5: SELF-SERVICE PORTAL EVOLUTION -->
    <outline text="🚀 Self-service portal: MVP success → architecture ceiling. 15K users, monolith can't support mobile or partner integrations">
      <outline text="Context: PHP monolith launched as MVP in 8 weeks, single MySQL database, no API layer, 200 new customers/month, sales promised mobile app and partner API for Q3, 4-person dev team, can't freeze features — revenue depends on it">

        <outline text="✅ Strangler fig pattern — extract services incrementally">
          <outline text="Wrap existing monolith behind a reverse proxy. Extract one capability at a time into new services. Old and new run side by side. Zero downtime migration.">
            <outline text="Trade-off: Slower than a rewrite initially. Team runs two architectures in parallel for 6-9 months. Requires discipline to not keep adding to the monolith." />
          </outline>

          <outline text="What to extract first?">
            <outline text="✅ API gateway — expose existing functionality via REST">
              <outline text="Build a thin API layer in front of the monolith. Mobile app and partners consume the API. Monolith serves the API instead of rendering HTML directly. Unblocks sales commitment in 6-8 weeks.">
                <outline text="Trade-off: API initially just proxies to monolith internals. Not a clean domain model yet. Will need refactoring as services are extracted behind it." />
              </outline>

              <outline text="How to handle data during the transition?">
                <outline text="✅ Shared database with API contracts">
                  <outline text="New API services read from the same MySQL database. But they only access data through well-defined internal APIs, never direct table access. This creates a seam for later extraction.">
                    <outline text="Trade-off: Database is still a single point of failure. Schema changes require coordination. But it avoids the data sync nightmare while the team is small." />
                  </outline>

                  <outline text="How to ship the mobile app while migrating?">
                    <outline text="✅ API-first: mobile and new web frontend consume the same API">
                      <outline text="Mobile app built against the same REST API. When the web frontend is eventually rewritten, it uses the same endpoints. One API, two consumers. Consistent behavior guaranteed." />
                      <outline text="Trade-off: API design must account for mobile constraints (payload size, offline support, battery). Adds up-front design effort. Mobile team blocked until core API endpoints are stable." />
                    </outline>
                    <outline text="❌ Separate backend-for-frontend (BFF) for mobile">
                      <outline text="Two backends to maintain with a 4-person team. Business logic duplicated or split across services. Bug fixes need to be applied twice. At 15K users this is unsustainable." />
                      <outline text="When it works: Large teams (10+ backend devs) where mobile and web have fundamentally different data needs and release cycles." />
                    </outline>
                    <outline text="❌ Delay mobile app until migration is complete">
                      <outline text="Migration takes 6-9 months. Sales promised mobile for Q3 — that's 14 weeks away. 200 customers/month are onboarding without mobile. Competitor already has an app. Losing €30K/month in churn." />
                      <outline text="When it works: Internal tools where there's no competitive pressure and quality matters more than speed." />
                    </outline>
                  </outline>
                </outline>

                <outline text="❌ Immediate database-per-service">
                  <outline text="With 4 developers, maintaining multiple databases, sync jobs, and distributed transactions is impossible. One failed sync and customer data is inconsistent across portal and mobile. Team spends more time on infrastructure than features." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Teams of 15+ with a dedicated platform/infrastructure group. Or when services have genuinely independent data with no cross-service queries." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="❌ Data sync via ETL pipeline">
                  <outline text="ETL introduces lag (5-30 min). Customer updates their profile on the portal, opens the mobile app, sees old data. Support tickets spike. ETL jobs fail silently on schema changes. At 200 new customers/month, data inconsistency erodes trust fast." />
                  <outline text="When it works: Analytics and reporting where near-real-time is acceptable. Or read-only data replication for dashboards and BI." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>

            <outline text="❌ Start with database splitting">
              <outline text="Database is the hardest part. Starting here means months of invisible infrastructure work with no user-facing value. Sales has nothing to show in Q3. Team morale drops working on plumbing. Meanwhile the monolith keeps growing." />
              <outline text="When it works: When the database is the bottleneck (connection limits, replication lag, storage costs). Here, it's the architecture that's the bottleneck, not the database." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="❌ Extract the UI first (new React frontend)">
              <outline text="A new frontend still calls the same monolith — same speed, same limitations. It's a cosmetic change that doesn't unlock mobile or partner integrations. 8-10 weeks of work that doesn't solve the API problem." />
              <outline text="When it works: When the backend is solid but the frontend is the user experience bottleneck (e.g., accessibility compliance, mobile-responsive redesign on a stable API)." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>

        <outline text="❌ Full rewrite (greenfield)">
          <outline text="6-12 months with a 4-person team. No new features during rewrite. 200 customers/month still onboarding to the old system — who migrates their data? Rewrites consistently take 2-3× longer than estimated. 70% of rewrites fail to ship." />
          <outline text="When it works: The existing codebase is truly unmaintainable (no tests, no documentation, original developers gone) AND you can afford to freeze features for 6+ months." />
        </outline>
        <outline text="❌ Keep the monolith and scale the team">
          <outline text="Hiring takes 3-4 months. New devs need 2 months to learn a codebase with no documentation. More developers on a monolith means more merge conflicts, longer deploy cycles, and slower releases. Brooks's Law: adding people to a late project makes it later." />
          <outline text="When it works: The monolith is well-structured (modular, tested, documented) and the bottleneck is genuinely capacity, not architecture." />
        </outline>

      </outline>
    </outline>

  </body>
</opml>
